ntdll: anonymous events closed right after they were made are kept for reuse; request counters.

D2R's protection thread makes an unnamed auto-reset event (initially set) and closes it
0.2 ms later, about 1,500 times a second (b150: tid 600, 1,534 pairs/s), and never waits
on it. Each pair costs two wineserver round trips (create_event, close_handle), so about
3,000 server calls a second; wineserver CPU went from 138 ms/s with that loop idle to
270 ms/s while it runs, on a device where every core is already short.

An event that NtCreateEvent makes with EVENT_ALL_ACCESS and no name, root directory,
security descriptor, quality of service or attribute other than OBJ_CASE_INSENSITIVE /
OBJ_OPENIF is noted as fresh (handle, making thread, type, time). When the thread that
made it closes it within 10 ms and nothing else can hold a reference to it, NtClose keeps
it open in a pool (16 per type) instead of sending close_handle. A fresh event is left out
when it is duplicated (NtDuplicateObject), given to an I/O call (NtReadFile, NtWriteFile,
the scatter/gather versions, NtDeviceIoControlFile, NtFsControlFile, NtLockFile,
NtNotifyChangeDirectoryFile, NtNotifyChangeMultipleKeys, socket event select), has its
handle flags changed (NtSetInformationObject) or is waited on by another thread. The next
plain NtCreateEvent of the same type takes a kept event back and sets its state with
NtSetEvent or NtResetEvent; the inproc sync fd stays cached, so no server call at all.
A handle closed again while in the pool really closes.

The unix side also counts every wineserver request by type, with its round trip time
(CLOCK_MONOTONIC from send to reply; a select includes its wait), and the event reuse
outcomes, in PE ntdll's export __wine_d2r_server_calls (struct d2r_server_calls, version
1, 8,256 bytes) for FEX. Section d2r_server_reqs of ntdll.so holds REQ_NB_REQUESTS so the
build can check the request name table it ships (share/wine/d2r-server-requests.txt).

WINE_D2R_EVENT_REUSE=0 closes every event as stock Wine; WINE_D2R_CALL_COUNTS=0 leaves
the counters off (version stays 0). Both are read once per process.
---
